 Let $f(x)=x^3+ax^2+ax+1.$  Since all the roots of $f(x)$ are real, $f(x)$ has three real roots, so we can write $f(x)=\prod_{i=1}^3(x-r_i),$ where $r_i$ are the roots of $f(x).$  Expanding this product and comparing coefficients, we have
\begin{align*}
x^3+\prod_{i=1}^3r_i x^2+\sum_{1\le i<j\le 3}r_ir_jx+\prod_{i=1}^3r_i &=x^3+ax^2+ax+1, \\
\prod_{i=1}^3r_i &=1, \\
\sum_{1\le i<j\le 3}r_ir_j &=a, \\
\prod_{i=1}^3r_i &=1.
\end{align*}
Since $a$ is positive, we have $\sum_{1\le i<j\le 3}r_ir_j>0.$  Thus, we must have $r_1r_2r_3>0,$ so all the roots have the same sign.  There are three cases to consider.  \paragraph{Case 1: All the roots are positive.}  Then $f(0)=1>0,$ so $f(x)$ is negative between the roots.  However, $f(-1)=1-a+1+a=2>0,$ which contradicts the fact that $f(x)$ is negative between the roots.  Thus, the roots cannot all be positive.  \paragraph{Case 2: All the roots are negative.}  Then $f(-1)=2>0,$ so $f(x)$ is positive between the roots.  However, $f(0)=1>0,$ which contradicts the fact that $f(x)$ is positive between the roots.  Thus, the roots cannot all be negative.  \paragraph{Case 3: The roots are two positive and one negative.}  Let $p$ be the negative root.  Then $f(x)=(x-p)(x^2+px+1).$  Expanding this product, we have
\begin{align*}
(x-p)(x^2+px+1) &=x^3+(p-1)x^2+(p-1)x-p \\
&=x^3+ax^2+ax+1,
\end{align*}
so $a=p-1.$  Since $p<0,$ we have $a<1.$  To find the smallest possible value of $a,$ we must maximize $p.$  However, $p$ cannot be too large, because $f(-1)=2>0,$ so $f(x)$ has a negative root between $-1$ and $0.$  Thus, $p<0.$  To maximize $p,$ we must have $f(-1)=0,$ so
\begin{align*}
(-1)^3+a(-1)^2+a(-1)+1 &=0, \\
-1+a-a+1 &=0, \\
0 &=0.
\end{align*}
Thus, $p=-1$ is the largest possible negative root.  Substituting $p=-1$ into $a=p-1,$ we have $a=-2.$  To check that this is indeed the smallest possible value of $a,$ let's consider the case where the roots are two negative and one positive.  Let $q$ be the positive root.  Then $f(x)=(x-q)(x^2+qx+1).$  Expanding this product, we have
\begin{align*}
(x-q)(x^2+qx+1) &=x^3+(q-1)x^2+(q-1)x-q \\
&=x^3+ax^2+ax+1,
\end{align*}
so $a=q-1.$  Since $q>0,$ we have $a>1.$  Thus, the smallest possible value of $a$ is $\boxed{-2}.$


