 We are given that $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ $d$ are distinct complex numbers with $|a| = |b| = |c| = |d| = 1$ and $a + b + c + d = 0.$  We want to find the maximum value of
\[|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)|.\]
Since $|a| = |b| = |c| = |d| = 1,$ we can write $a = e^{i\alpha},$ $b = e^{i\beta},$ $c = e^{i\gamma},$ and $d = e^{i\delta}$ for some angles $\alpha,$ $\beta,$ $\gamma,$ and $\delta.$ Then
\begin{align*}
a + b + c + d &= e^{i\alpha} + e^{i\beta} + e^{i\gamma} + e^{i\delta} \\
&= 2\cos\left(\frac{\alpha - \beta}{2}\right)e^{i\frac{\alpha + \beta}{2}} + 2\cos\left(\frac{\gamma - \delta}{2}\right)e^{i\frac{\gamma + \delta}{2}} \\
&= 2\left[\cos\left(\frac{\alpha - \beta}{2}\right)e^{i\frac{\alpha + \beta}{2}} + \cos\left(\frac{\gamma - \delta}{2}\right)e^{i\frac{\gamma + \delta}{2}}\right].
\end{align*}
Since $a + b + c + d = 0,$ we have
\begin{align*}
2\left[\cos\left(\frac{\alpha - \beta}{2}\right)e^{i\frac{\alpha + \beta}{2}} + \cos\left(\frac{\gamma - \delta}{2}\right)e^{i\frac{\gamma + \delta}{2}}\right] &= 0 \\
\cos\left(\frac{\alpha - \beta}{2}\right)e^{i\frac{\alpha + \beta}{2}} &= -\cos\left(\frac{\gamma - \delta}{2}\right)e^{i\frac{\gamma + \delta}{2}}.
\end{align*}
This implies that
\begin{align*}
\left|\cos\left(\frac{\alpha - \beta}{2}\right)e^{i\frac{\alpha + \beta}{2}}\right| &= \left|\cos\left(\frac{\gamma - \delta}{2}\right)e^{i\frac{\gamma + \delta}{2}}\right| \\
\left|\cos\left(\frac{\alpha - \beta}{2}\right)\right| &= \left|\cos\left(\frac{\gamma - \delta}{2}\right)\right|.
\end{align*}
Now, consider the product
\begin{align*}
|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)| &= |a + b| \cdot |a + c| \cdot |a + d| \cdot |b + c| \cdot |b + d| \cdot |c + d| \\
&= |e^{i\alpha} + e^{i\beta}| \cdot |e^{i\alpha} + e^{i\gamma}| \cdot |e^{i\alpha} + e^{i\delta}| \cdot |e^{i\beta} + e^{i\gamma}| \cdot |e^{i\beta} + e^{i\delta}| \cdot |e^{i\gamma} + e^{i\delta}| \\
&= 2^{3} \cdot \left|\cos\left(\frac{\alpha - \beta}{2}\right)\right| \cdot \left|\cos\left(\frac{\alpha - \gamma}{2}\right)\right| \cdot \left|\cos\left(\frac{\alpha - \delta}{2}\right)\right| \\
&\quad \cdot \left|\cos\left(\frac{\beta - \gamma}{2}\right)\right| \cdot \left|\cos\left(\frac{\beta - \delta}{2}\right)\right| \cdot \left|\cos\left(\gamma - \delta\right)\right|.
\end{align*}
Since $|a| = |b| = |c| = |d| = 1,$ we have $\cos\left(\frac{\alpha - \beta}{2}\right) = \cos\left(\frac{\gamma - \delta}{2}\right) = \cos\left(\frac{\alpha - \gamma}{2}\right) = \cos\left(\frac{\beta - \gamma}{2}\right) = \cos\left(\frac{\beta - \delta}{2}\right) = \cos\left(\gamma - \delta\right) = 1.$ Therefore,
\begin{align*}
|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)| &= 2^{3} \cdot 1 \cdot 1 \cdot 1 \cdot 1 \cdot 1 \\
&= 8.
\end{align*}
Thus, the maximum value of $|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)|$ is $\boxed{8}.$The answer is: \boxed{8}

