We can expand $(\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{3})^3$ as follows:
\begin{align*}
(\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{3})^3 &= (\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{3})(\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{3})(\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{3}) \\
&= (\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{3})(2+2\sqrt{2}+3+3\sqrt{3}) \\
&= (\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{3})(5+2\sqrt{2}+3\sqrt{3}) \\
&= 5\sqrt{2} + 2(\sqrt{2})^2 + 3\sqrt{2}\sqrt{3} + 5\sqrt{3} + 2\sqrt{3}(\sqrt{2}) + 3(\sqrt{3})^2 \\
&= 5\sqrt{2} + 2(2) + 3\sqrt{6} + 5\sqrt{3} + 2\sqrt{6} + 3(3) \\
&= 5\sqrt{2} + 4 + 3\sqrt{6} + 5\sqrt{3} + 2\sqrt{6} + 9 \\
&= 13 + 5\sqrt{3} + 5\sqrt{2} + 5\sqrt{6}
\end{align*}
Comparing this to the form $a\sqrt{2} + b\sqrt{3} + c\sqrt{6}$, we have $a=5$, $b=5$, and $c=5$.
Therefore, $a+b+c = 5+5+5 = \boxed{15}$.The answer is: \boxed{15}
